seven men are trying to become iran's  eighth president  the presidential elections due to take  place on june 18th  now the moderate candidate abdul nasir  hamati who is the former head of iran  central bank  has been running on the campaign promise  of trying to fix  iran's failing economy as a result of  crippling u.s sanctions  since the united states withdrew from  the nuclear deal in 2018  hassan rouhani took office eight years  ago and since then the economy has  really  plummeted in this country the  unemployment rate is at an  all-time high the real has lost over 80  percent of its value  that is where the moderate candidate is  really focusing his attention  to try and convince voters to vote for  him because he  has the expertise the financial  background to try and fix the country's  economy  meanwhile the frontrunner in the race is  the conservative candidate ibrahim raisi  who is currently the head of iran's  judiciary now  ibrahim raisi has been really the front  runner because we've seen  the support of the establishment very  much behind him  he was appointed to the head of the  judiciary by the supreme leader aita  ali khamenei and he is still in that  position if he becomes president he will  resign from that  post his promises has been that he will  continue negotiations in vienna  to try and reach an agreement to the  nuclear deal and try to keep that going  he ran in 2017 against president hassan  rouhani he managed to get nearly 16  million votes  but lost to the president at that time  voter turnout is of high importance to  the establishment here the lowest voter  turnout they had  in this country since the revolution of  1979  was around 50 percent and that was in  the presidential elections of 1993.  there is a sense that the people are  very much disillusioned with  the governments that they vote for a lot  of people i've spoken to in the capital  explained that they just can't be  bothered to vote this time because they  don't see much difference in the  policies  of the various candidates the main point  of contention has been that  the moderates and the conservatives that  they've had in government over the past  few years  haven't really made much of a difference  in the day-to-day lives of the people  and that is because  iran's foreign policy is not decided at  the level of the government it is  decided at the level  of the system and therefore it is not  really up to  a president how the country will have  its interactions with the outside world  that is decided on a much broader  and higher level for now the system is  encouraging people to vote  that it is their moral duty it is the  duty of every iranian to come out on  june 18th  but there is a sense that any kind of a  turnout over 40  will be seen as a victory for this  system
 

